# Coverage configuration for the tool's own test suite.
#
# Why measure this at all: the tool's own argument about the tests it generates
# is that "it ran" is not "it was checked". The project had no number for itself
# at all, which is the gap this closes.
#
# Worth being precise about what it is, though — coverage is the *weaker* of the
# two metrics. This repo's headline for generated tests is mutation score
# (Phase 8); line coverage is only Phase 6. So this does not put the suite on
# the same footing as the tests it measures. Mutmut or cosmic-ray over these
# modules would, and that is a much larger job.
#
# Scope, stated plainly — the number is easy to misread, so:
#
#   * It is measured in the `test` CI job, which deselects the e2e tests. So it
#     under-reports by design: agent.py, compiler.py and report_generator.py are
#     driven mainly by the end-to-end test and score far higher with it
#     included.
#   * It covers everything that ships, not just the flat modules — hence
#     extractor/ and main.py being in the table.
#   * Three entries are low for known reasons, none of them "nobody got round to
#     it": extractor/sootup_backend.py is the opt-in legacy backend that is
#     documented to degrade gracefully and is not exercised at all;
#     main.py scores 0% here only because the offline suite never invokes the
#     CLI, while the `docker` job does run `analyze` through it; and
#     llm_client.py's missing lines are the real API calls, which the offline
#     suite is built never to make.
#
# There is deliberately no --cov-fail-under. A floor low enough not to be
# tripped by Windows-vs-Linux branch differences ("if sys.platform == win32"
# runs one arm or the other) would be too low to catch anything, and a
# meaningful one would push towards re-testing offline what e2e already covers.
[run]
source = .
omit =
    tests/*
    setup.py
    skill_entry.py

[report]
show_missing = True
